{"about":"66df759e09c49faedd7f1b9e7ebf1d8ceeac2fed60fcd2cbf4ca3d2592289264","body":"The conjecture attempts to solve the problem of preference by invoking 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness), but Popper's own formal attempt to define this concept was proven logically untenable by Tichý and Miller in 1974. They demonstrated that within Popper's framework, a false theory with more content cannot be strictly said to be closer to the truth than a false theory with less content; thus, appealing to verisimilitude does not escape the logical vacuum left by the rejection of induction, but merely papers over it with an intuitive but formally undefined notion.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The reliance on verisimilitude"}